course: biology
lesson: 332
title: Lost pets, microchips, holidays and moving house
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A lost pet cannot tell anyone where it lives, so people have given pets ways to "speak" for them: a tag you can read with your eyes and a tiny chip you can read with a scanner.
explain: At the vet's, a microchip about the size of a grain of rice slides under the skin between a puppy's shoulder blades. It has no battery and never runs out; it just holds a 15-digit number that links to the owner's name and address on a list.
explain: If the dog runs off on Bonfire Night and a stranger finds it shaking in a park, a vet waves a scanner over its back, reads the number, and the dog can be home that same evening, as long as the owner kept the details up to date.
explain: Pets also get lost in other ways: a cat let out too soon after moving house may wander back to the old home, and a scared cat hides silently in a shed instead of answering its name.
fact: A microchip is about the size of a grain of rice and is placed under the skin between the shoulder blades.
fact: A scanner held close reads the chip's number, which links to the owner's details on a list, so owners must update their details when they move.
fact: Most microchips carry a 15-digit number that no other animal shares; the chip has no battery, lasts for life, and cannot track a pet like a GPS tracker.
fact: In Britain dogs must be microchipped by law, and since 2024 pet cats must be too.
fact: In Britain a dog out in public must wear a collar tag with its owner's name and address; cats wear safety collars that snap open if they catch on a branch.
fact: Lost cats often hide close by, quiet and scared, in sheds, garages and under cars, so checking neighbours' sheds, putting up posters, calling vets and calling at quiet times help.
fact: Bobbie the Wonder Dog was reported to walk about 4,000 kilometres home across the USA in 1924, and scientists still do not know how some pets find their way home.
fact: Kennels, catteries, pet sitters or friends can look after pets during holidays; kennels ask for proof of vaccinations, and a sitter lets a pet stay calm in its own home.
fact: To cross many borders pets need a microchip, a rabies vaccine and papers, and dogs coming into Britain also need a worm treatment; the rules keep diseases like rabies out.
fact: After moving house a cat is kept inside for a couple of weeks to learn its new home; butter on the paws does not help, it just makes greasy paws.
fact: More pets go missing around fireworks than at almost any other time, because a dog's hearing is much sharper than ours; keeping pets inside with curtains closed and a den helps.
fact: A found dog should not be grabbed, because a scared dog may bite; a grown-up can call the dog warden or take it to a vet, who scans the chip for free.
exercise: How big is a pet microchip? | About the size of a grain of rice | About the size of a mobile phone, so it fits in a pocket | About the size of a large round coin | About the size of a box of matches
exercise: Where on a dog is a microchip usually put? | Under the skin between the shoulder blades | In the tip of the tail, where it cannot be felt or chewed | Inside one ear | Under one of the back paws
exercise: How is the number on a microchip read? | By a scanner held close to the pet | By a satellite circling far out in space | By looking closely at the pet's fur | By an app that finds the pet anywhere
exercise: Why must owners update their details when they move house? | The chip number links to their details on a list | The chip stops working in a new town until the vet turns it on again | The chip has to be swapped for a new one | The vet must take the chip out
exercise: How many digits does a pet microchip number usually have? | 15 | 4 | 8 | 30
exercise: What is the difference between a microchip and a GPS tracker? | A chip only holds a number to scan; GPS shows where the pet is | A chip shows the pet on a map on your phone, but GPS only holds a number | Both show the pet on a map all the time | A chip is bigger than a GPS tracker
exercise: Which pets must be microchipped by law in Britain? | Dogs, and since 2024 cats too | Only racehorses and show ponies | Only dogs that are bigger than a sheep | Goldfish, budgies and hamsters
exercise: What must a dog's collar tag show in Britain? | Its owner's name and address | The vet's phone number and the dog's birthday | The date of the dog's birthday | The dog's favourite food and toy
exercise: Why do cat collars snap open by themselves? | So the cat is not trapped if the collar catches on a branch | So the cat can take it off by itself to sleep more comfortably at night | So the tag can be read faster | So the cat cannot be stroked
exercise: Where do lost cats often hide? | Close by, quietly, in sheds, garages and under cars | Far away in the middle of town | High in the tallest tree in the park | At the vet's waiting room
exercise: What was Bobbie the Wonder Dog famous for? | He was reported to walk about 4,000 kilometres home across the USA | He swam across the whole Atlantic Ocean to find his family in Europe | He found a lost cat in every town | He flew home on a plane by himself
exercise: Who can look after a pet while a family is on holiday? | Kennels, catteries, pet sitters or friends | Nobody; pets must always come along | The pet itself, with a big bowl of food | The postman, on his rounds
exercise: What do pets need to cross many borders? | A microchip, a rabies vaccine and papers | A ticket and a seat by the window, like any passenger | Only a passport photo | Only a new collar
exercise: What helps a cat learn its new home after moving? | Keeping it inside for a couple of weeks | Putting butter on its paws | Letting it out on the first night | Driving it round the new street
exercise: When do more pets go missing than at almost any other time? | Around fireworks | On sunny Sunday mornings | During the school holidays | When it snows lightly
exercise: You find a scared dog alone in the park. What should you do? | Not grab it, and tell a grown-up who can get its chip scanned | Grab its collar and run home with it, then put up posters in the street | Chase it until it stops | Leave it some chocolate and walk away
test: «just put a tracker chip in the cat, now i can see where she goes on my phone right» What is right? | No, a microchip only holds a number a scanner reads up close | Yes, every microchip sends a map of where she is to your phone | Yes, but only at night | No, the chip only works on dogs
test: «we moved ages ago, never bothered changing the chip details, whatever» What would happen if the dog got lost? | The chip would lead the finder to the old address | The chip would update itself | The dog could not be scanned at all | The chip would ring the owner's phone
test: «cat's been gone 2 days, bloody hell, she's probably miles away» What is a good first step? | Search neighbours' sheds and garages and call quietly at night | Drive to the next city and shout her name as loudly as you can from the car | Wait a month before doing anything | Buy a new cat straight away
test: Why might a scared cat not come when you call its name? | Scared cats tend to hide silently | Cats forget their names in two days | Cats cannot hear quiet human voices | Cats will only ever answer to whistles
test: Grandad says: «butter the cat's paws when you move, then she'll stay.» What is right? | It does not help; keep her inside for a couple of weeks instead | It works, but only if you butter all four paws twice with salted butter | It works on every cat | It works only with salted butter
test: «fireworks tonight, gonna take the dog to watch» What is the best answer? | Keep him inside with the curtains shut and a den; bangs sound louder to him | Take him, but cover his eyes with a scarf so the flashes do not scare him at all | Dogs love fireworks, take him | Leave him tied up in the garden
test: Why might a cat prefer a pet sitter at home to a cattery? | It can stay calm in its own home | Catteries never feed cats | Sitters let cats go outside on holiday | Cats cannot sleep in a cattery
test: Why do countries make rules about animals coming in? | To keep diseases like rabies out | To stop pets eating any foreign food | To count how many pets are on holiday | To make pets learn a new language
test: A cartoon dog walks home across a whole country and a friend says it is all made up. What is true? | Some real pets have found their way home, and scientists still do not know how | All lost dogs find their way home within a day, because they follow their own smell | Pets cannot find their way across one street | Only cartoon dogs can find their way home
test: «there's a dog in the park, no owner, it's wagging, i'll just grab it» What is the risk? | Even a friendly scared dog may bite if grabbed | Wagging dogs never bite | The dog will run home by itself in a second | Touching a dog's collar breaks its chip
test: Count them: at the kennels there are 4 dogs and 2 cats in the cattery next door. How many ears and how many tails are there? | 12 ears and 6 tails | 6 ears and 6 tails | 12 ears and 4 tails | 24 ears and 6 tails
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a microchip implanted in a domestic animal? | It bears a unique number, readable by a scanner at close range, linked to the owner's registered details | It transmits the position of the animal continuously to a satellite, permitting it to be traced upon a map | It records the sounds heard by the animal | It requires replacement of its battery at annual intervals
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an owner is required to amend the details registered against a microchip upon a change of residence. | The microchip number is linked to those details, which alone permit the return of the animal | The microchip ceases to function in a different postal district | The law requires a new microchip at each change of address | The microchip is otherwise unreadable by any scanner
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the retention of a cat indoors following a change of residence. | Confinement for a period of approximately two weeks assists the animal in learning its new home | The application of butter to the paws renders confinement unnecessary | Immediate release on the first night is recommended | Cats are incapable of learning a new home under any conditions
exam: Ascertain the conduct appropriate for a child who discovers an unaccompanied dog in a public park. | Refraining from seizing the animal and informing an adult, who may arrange for the scanning of its microchip | Seizing the animal by the collar and removing it to the child's own residence, there to await its owner | Pursuing the animal until it is exhausted | Feeding the animal with confectionery in order to calm it
